*.log
.DS_Store
.env
.env.backup
.env.production

# Every VARIANT, not just the exact names above. Eleven .env.backup-* files holding production
# secrets sat untracked and unignored, because `.env.backup` matches only a file called exactly
# that - never `.env.backup-before-google-oauth-change`. One `git add -A` would have pushed them
# to GitHub. Added 2026-08-31. The `!client/.env.production` exception below still wins, being later.
.env.*
# .env.example is a TEMPLATE and is committed on purpose - it lists the variables the server needs
# with placeholder values so a new machine can be set up without guessing. It is already tracked, so
# the rule above does not affect it today; this exception makes that deliberate rather than luck, and
# keeps it working if the file is ever removed and re-added.
!server/.env.example
# client/.env.production is committed on purpose: it holds no secrets, and keeping it in the
# repository makes a production build correct on any machine instead of depending on someone
# creating the file first. Keep this exception.
!client/.env.production
/.fleet
/.idea
/.nova
/.vscode
/.zed
/node_modules
Thumbs.db

# Backup sets: database dumps and copies of every uploaded file. Large, and they contain
# client identification and commission figures — never in version control.
backups/

# Dead Laravel leftovers that may still sit on an old working copy: compiled Blade view
# caches and the PHP log. Nothing in the NestJS backend reads them (it uses storage/app
# only). Ignored so they can never be committed by accident; safe to delete locally.
storage/framework/
storage/logs/

# Playwright run artefacts — regenerated on every run.
test-results/
e2e/test-results/
e2e/playwright-report/

# The browser suite's remembered TEST_DATABASE_URL. It is a connection string WITH THE PASSWORD in
# it, and it sat outside every rule above — `.env` is ignored, this is not an `.env`. On a public
# repository one `git add -A` publishes the database account, and a pushed credential is not undone
# by deleting it: it stays in history and in every fork. Local convenience only; never shared.
e2e/.testdb

# QA restore rehearsals. `backups/` was ignored and this sibling was not, so an 83 MB pg_dump of the
# QA database — real leads, message bodies, user rows and password hashes — sat one `git add -A`
# away from the history. A pushed dump is not undone by deleting it: it stays in every clone and
# every fork. Same reasoning as the line above.
backups-qa/

# --- local deploy backups: never commit ---
dist-backup-*/
server-backups/
server_backup_*/
*.backup
*.backup-*
*.before-*
